

1. Core idea

Input: ResolvedScenario (kernel_graph + tensor_pool).

Process: Step through kernels K0, K1, K2…, apply ops on T0, T1, T2….

Output: Updated tensor pool over discrete timesteps—ready to be either visualized or shipped to a real backend later.

2. Minimal simulator loop (pseudo-Python)

class BackendSimulator:
    def __init__(self, kernel_graph, tensor_pool):
        self.kernel_graph = kernel_graph
        self.tensor_pool = tensor_pool

    def step(self, dt):
        for k in self.kernel_graph.kernels_in_order():
            self._execute_kernel(k)

    def _execute_kernel(self, k):
        if k.op_code == "AXPY":
            self._axpy(k)
        elif k.op_code == "STENCIL_3D":
            self._stencil_3d(k)
        # ... more opcodes

    def _axpy(self, k):
        x = self.tensor_pool[k.inputs[0].id]
        y = self.tensor_pool[k.inputs[1].id]
        alpha = k.params["alpha"]
        out = self.tensor_pool[k.outputs[0].id]
        out[:] = alpha * x + y

    def _stencil_3d(self, k):
        src = self.tensor_pool[k.inputs[0].id]
        dst = self.tensor_pool[k.outputs[0].id]
        # apply simple 3D laplacian or similar mock stencil
        # (implementation detail can stay abstract for now)

No physics. No semantics. Just opcodes and arrays.

3. Why this is the right next step

You get a closed loop: .cmb → ResolvedScenario → kernel graph → evolving tensors.

